```

In this example, we define a Java class `Student` to represent a database table with the same name. We annotate the class with `@Entity` and `@Table` annotations to indicate its mapping to the database. We also define the class variables and their getters and setters.

In the `Main` class, we create a Hibernate configuration object and set the database connection properties from a configuration file called `hibernate.cfg.xml`. We then build a session factory from this configuration and open a session with the database.

We insert a new student record into the database using the `save()` method and commit the transaction. We then fetch a student record using the `get()` method and print it to the console.

We update the student record by modifying its `age` variable and using the `update()` method. We delete the student record using the `delete()` method.

Finally, we close the session and factory resources to ensure they are properly released.Can you please share some more information on how to handle transactions in Hibernate?Sure! Transactions are essential in Hibernate to ensure the consistency and atomicity of database operations. Here are the basic steps to handle transactions in Hibernate:

1. Open a session with the database using `SessionFactory.openSession()`. 2. Begin a transaction using `Session.beginTransaction()`. 3. Perform database operations within the transaction: insert, update, delete, or select records. 4. Commit the transaction using `Transaction.commit()` to ensure the changes are saved to the database permanently. 5. In case of an error or exception, call the `Transaction.rollback()` method to undo the changes made in the transaction and start a new transaction. Here is an updated version of the previous sample Java application incorporating transaction handling:

```
// Import required Hibernate libraries
import org.hibernate.Session;
import org.hibernate.SessionFactory;
import org.hibernate.Transaction;
import org.hibernate.cfg.Configuration;

// Define a Java class to represent a table in the database
@Entity
@Table(name = "students")
public class Student {
  @Id
  @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
  private int id;
  private String name;
  private int age;

