Implement a Scala program that finds the shortest substring in a string containing all given charactersHere is a possible implementation of the program in Scala:

```scala
object ShortestSubstring {

  def findShortestSubstring(s: String, chars: Set[Char]): Option[String] = {
    var left = 0
    var right = 0
    var count = 0
    var minLen = Int.MaxValue
    var minStart = 0
    val seen = collection.mutable.Map.empty[Char, Int].withDefaultValue(0)

    while (right < s.length) {
      val c = s(right)
      if (chars.contains(c)) {
        seen(c) += 1
        if (seen(c) == 1) {
          count += 1
        }
      }
      right += 1

      while (count == chars.size) {
        if (right - left < minLen) {
          minLen = right - left
          minStart = left
        }

        val c = s(left)
        if (chars.contains(c)) {
          seen(c) -= 1
          if (seen(c) == 0) {
            count -= 1
          }
        }
        left += 1
      }
    }

    if (minLen == Int.MaxValue) {
      None
    } else {
      Some(s.substring(minStart, minStart + minLen))
    }
  }

  def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = {
    val s = "abcdefghi"
    val chars = Set('b', 'e', 'i')
    val result = findShortestSubstring(s, chars)
    println(result)
  }
}
```

The `findShortestSubstring` function takes a string `s` and a set of characters `chars`, and returns an optional string that is the shortest substring of `s` that contains all the characters in `chars`. If no such substring exists, it returns `None`.

The function uses a sliding window approach with two pointers (`left` and `right`) and a data structure to keep track of the counts of characters seen so far (`seen`). At each step, it advances `right` and updates the counts and the `count` variable (which keeps track of the number of distinct characters seen so far). When `count` reaches the size of `chars`, it advances `left` until `count` decreases, keeping track of the minimum length and start position of a valid substring encountered so far. Finally, it returns the corresponding substring if found.

The `main` function provides an example usage of the function with a string "abcdefghi" and a set of characters Set('b', 'e', 'i'), and prints the result (which should be "bei" in this case).How can I modify it to handle duplicates of characters in the input string and character set?To handle duplicates of characters in the input string and character set, you can modify the `seen` variable to be a `Map[Char, Set[Int]]` that keeps track of the positions where each character was seen instead of the number of times. Here's the modified implementation:

```scala
object ShortestSubstring {

  def findShortestSubstring(s: String, chars: Set[Char]): Option[String] = {
    var left = 0
    var right = 0
    var count = 0
    var minLen = Int.MaxValue
    var minStart = 0
    val seen = collection.mutable.Map.empty[Char, Set[Int]].withDefaultValue(Set.empty)

    while (right < s.length) {
      val c = s(right)
      if (chars.contains(c)) {
        val pos = seen(c) + right
        seen(c) = pos
        if (pos.size == 1) {
          count += 1
        }
      }
      right += 1

      while (count == chars.size) {
        if (right - left < minLen) {
          minLen = right - left
          minStart = left
        }

        val c = s(left)
        if (chars.contains(c)) {
          val pos = seen(c)
          seen(c) = pos - left
          if (pos.size == 1) {
            count -= 1
          }
        }
        left += 1
      }
    }

    if (minLen == Int.MaxValue) {
      None
    } else {
      Some(s.substring(minStart, minStart + minLen))
    }
  }

  def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = {
    val s = "abcbca"
    val chars = Set('a', 'b', 'c')
    val result = findShortestSubstring(s, chars)
    println(result)
  }
}
```

The `seen` map now maps each character to a set of positions (represented as integer offsets from the start of the string) instead of counts. When a character is seen, its position is added to its set, and the count is incremented only if the set size becomes 1 (which means it was not seen before). When `count` reaches the size of `chars`, `left` is advanced until `count` decreases, keeping track of the minimum length and start position of a valid substring encountered so far. When a character is removed from the sliding window, its position is updated by subtracting the left offset from it, and the count is decremented only if the set size becomes 0 (which means it was removed from the sliding window). The `main` function provides an example usage of the function with a string "abcbca" and a set of characters Set('a', 'b', 'c'), and prints the result (which should be "abc" in this case). Note that the function returns the shortest substring that contains all characters, even if it includes duplicates of some of them.Great, this modification makes sense!I'm glad I could help. Don't hesitate to reach out if you have any more questions or if there's anything else I can do for you.
